          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have had a cough for two weeks after a cold.",
          "answer": "A cough that lingers for two to three weeks after a cold is common. The reference summary \"Upper respiratory infection (common cold)\" says cough often outlasts the other symptoms, and that this is normal and not by itself a sign of a bacterial infection. The antibiotic stewardship guidance says the same: a cough persisting two to three weeks after a cold is expected.\n\nThe red-flag check did not find an emergency pattern in what you wrote. That does not mean nothing is wrong. The check only covers a fixed list of presentations.\n\nYou should see a clinician soon if any of these apply:\n- The cough is getting worse instead of slowly easing, or you feel better and then get suddenly worse. The influenza summary notes that a return of illness after several days of improvement can be a sign of a secondary bacterial pneumonia.\n- You have shortness of breath, chest pain, a high fever, or you are coughing up blood.\n- The cough is still going on after about three weeks, or it comes with wheezing or tightness in the chest, especially at night or after exercise. The asthma reference notes that cough alone, particularly at night, can be an early sign of asthma.\n\nIf you have any of the serious signs above, especially trouble breathing or chest pain, seek urgent care now rather than waiting.\n\nThe sources I checked do not cover what treatment would suit you, and I can't advise on medicines. A clinician can examine you and decide whether anything more is needed.\n\nSources: Upper respiratory infection (common cold); Influenza; Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship guidance; Asthma; Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and stepwise approach.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
