          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Would antibiotics help?",
          "answer": "Based on the antibiotic stewardship guidance (\"Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship and when antibiotics are indicated\"), antibiotics generally do not help a cold or the cough that follows it. That guidance says these infections are viral in most cases, and antibiotics do not shorten them. It also notes that antibiotics can cause harm, such as side effects, allergic reactions, and resistance. The guidance describes not prescribing antibiotics for these cases as the recommended approach.\n\nThe same guidance says exceptions exist, but the passage I retrieved did not list them in detail. So I cannot say whether your situation would be one of them. The advice above about when to see a clinician still applies. If your cough gets worse, you feel better and then get suddenly ill again, or you develop shortness of breath, chest pain, or a high fever, a clinician should assess you. Whether antibiotics are needed is a decision for them to make after examining you.\n\nI did not find anything in the retrieved sources on which specific medicines to use, and I cannot advise on medicines.\n\nSources: Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship and when antibiotics are indicated; Influenza: annual vaccination and the timing of antiviral treatment (which states antibiotics have no activity against influenza).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
